<h1 id="a0000000001" class="tex-chapter"><span data-tag="">8</span> Appendix</h1>

<p>
This appendix is comprised of three parts. In §<a href="/tag/D1DCA9" data-tag="D1DCA9">8.1</a>, we will review some ideas from classical category theory, such as monoidal structures, enriched categories, and Quillen's small object argument. We give a brief overview of the theory of model categories in §<a href="/tag/0CE232" data-tag="0CE232">8.2</a>. The main result here is Proposition <a href="/tag/8C7DD0" data-tag="8C7DD0">8.2.6.13</a>, which will allow us to establish the existence of model category structures in a variety of situations with a minimal amount of effort. In §<a href="/tag/A3B2D6" data-tag="A3B2D6">8.3</a> we will use this result to make detailed study of the theory of simplicial categories. Our exposition is rather dense; for a more leisurely account of the theory of model categories we refer the reader to one of the standard texts, such as <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/hovey">hovey</a>]</span>. 
</p>
